This module covers the services that help devices obtain IP addresses and communicate with other devices on a network. I learned about DHCP, DNS, and their roles in network communication.

Topics Covered
Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP)
DHCP server and client
How DHCP assigns IP addresses
DHCP address assignment process
Domain Name System (DNS)
DNS name resolution
Static and dynamic IP addressing

What I Learned
DHCP automatically assigns IP configuration to devices.
DHCP can provide an IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, and DNS server address.
A DHCP client requests network configuration from a DHCP server.
DHCP uses the DORA process: Discover, Offer, Request, and Acknowledgment.
DNS translates domain names into IP addresses.
DNS helps users access websites without remembering their IP addresses.
Static IP addresses are configured manually, while dynamic IP addresses are usually assigned automatically through DHCP.

DHCP: When a computer connects to a network, it can automatically receive an IP address from a DHCP server.
DNS: When a user enters www.cisco.com in a browser, DNS helps find the IP address associated with that domain name.
